  "slug": "ai-topic-c4ec",
  "summary": "Audits of STATE, GEARS and plate design question perturbation benchmarks while new models add disentanglement, context and measured-experiment memory.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is whether single-cell perturbation response predictions are reliable and how evaluation and representation choices shape that answer. A STATE audit finds a low-rank attention bottleneck bounds decoder reliability. The Delta Gap preprint shows global Pearson inflates performance, with GEARS at delta Pearson 0.076 versus 0.267 for a mean-delta baseline on Replogle K562. Plate-design analysis finds plate layout and well effects confound donor variation and benchmark evaluation, and proposes design-aware estimators. Alongside these audits, models separate confounding factors or use measured data: scDisentangle and PertVI disentangle attributes, cell state, batch and treatment; UnDetect separates context from compound effects; BioMeme replaces a learned state-to-response layer with a memory of measured experiments; scBIG++ models across genes and cells. Audits now accompany architectures that make context explicit.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-12"
}
